※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ **A reference handed to a call is gone when the call is**, where what the call answers cannot
※ hold one: nothing is left that could reach the place through it.  So one expression may lend
※ the same place to several calls in turn -- `⍕` of three values into one pool, joined -- and
※ that is three references one after another, not three at once.
※
※ An answer that may hold a reference keeps what it was handed: until the statement ends, or
※ for as long as the name it is bound to lives.
※
※ Compare: **Rust**, whose borrows end where they are last used, which for an argument of a
※ call whose result has no lifetime tied to it is the end of the call.

let std := ⎕import("std")

@[impure]
fn bump(n: &mut u8) → u8:
    n⌖ ← n⌖ + 1u8
    n⌖

fn three(a: u16, b: u16, c: u16) → bool:
    let pool: mut arena = ⎕arena
    defer ⎕empty(pool)
    let t: str = ((⍕a in pool) ⧺ (⍕b in pool) in pool) ⧺ (⍕c in pool) in pool
    t = "123"

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let n: mut u8 = 0u8
    if bump(&mut n) + bump(&mut n) ≠ 3u8:
        return 1u6
    if ¬three(1u16, 2u16, 3u16):
        return 2u6
    0u6
